name  = binarytrees
shape = allocate a great many small nodes, walk them, discard them
args  = 16
claim = the shape the region model is built around, and the one where its two
        halves pull in opposite directions. Allocation is a bump -- no header to
        fill in, no free list to consult, no collector to trace -- so the TIME
        should be good; and nothing is ever handed back, so every discarded tree
        stays resident and the FOOTPRINT should be bad. A row that splits is
        worth more than one that wins.
        The C row frees each tree after checking it, which is what a C
        programmer writes and is why its footprint stays flat. A C version that
        bump-allocated into an arena and never freed would look like the Mere
        row -- that is the comparison Mere is making, not a different algorithm.
        bench_region.mere puts each depth's batch inside a `region R { }` block,
        which is the language's answer, and pays a copy of the answer out.

# Total allocation across every arena, at v0.1.322 -- 175,638,224 B, down from
# 353,371,856 B in v0.1.321. That halving is what shared nullary constructors buy:
# 50.3% of what it used to allocate was `Leaf`, a constructor with no payload,
# and every one of those 7.4 million empty nodes was a fresh 24 bytes. They
# share one static now (v0.1.322). The floor below CAUGHT the change as a breach, which is
# what a floor is for -- a number that drops by half is either an improvement or
# a meter that stopped looking, and the two are indistinguishable from the
# outside.
#
# The two Mere rows still sit at the same point to the byte (175,638,224 for
# both), which is the other finding: a `region R { }` whose result is an int
# copies almost nothing out, so bounding the residency is FREE here. What it
# changes is peak. Contrast churn, where the same idea costs 44% MORE
# cumulative allocation, because a loop has to deep-copy its live set into
# every new generation and a block only has to copy its answer.
# 117,092,664 B at v0.1.419: a Branch node is 16 bytes instead of 24 -- the tag
# moved into the pointer's low bits (Q-104), so the node is the payload alone.
# Two thirds of the previous number, to the byte. The floor caught the change
# as a breach again, which is its job; it moves down with the layout.
alloc_min = 100663296
alloc_max = 134217728

# The number the region block exists to control. 32,505,856 B at v0.1.322,
# halved along with everything else; the per-tree row's arena stays at the
# 1 MiB seed from v0.1.419 (a depth-16 tree is 1.0 MB of 16-byte nodes). bench_pertree.mere draws the block around
# one tree instead of one batch and peaks at 3,145,728 B (a depth-16 tree is
# 1.5 MB of nodes, so the 1 MiB seed grows once); its peak RSS, 5.5-5.8 MiB,
# is the Rust row's. The ceiling stays where the batch row needs it.
peak_max = 50331648

# MoonBit needs strconv for the depth argument.
mbt_imports = moonbitlang/core/env moonbitlang/core/strconv
